# Aerodynamics, aircraft systems and flight instruments: PHAK (FAA-H-8083-25C) chapters 5, 7 and 8; Airplane Flying Handbook (FAA-H-8083-3C). ACS areas I.G, I.H and VIII.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 1, "Stalls", "An airplane wing stalls when",
      ["it exceeds its critical angle of attack", "its airspeed falls below the bottom of the green arc", "its pitch attitude is above 20 degrees"],
      "A wing stalls at its critical angle of attack, whatever the airspeed, attitude or weight. Stall speeds on the airspeed indicator only apply in particular conditions."),
    S(1, 2, "Load factor", "In a level, coordinated 60-degree banked turn, what load factor does the airplane experience?",
      ["2 G", "1.5 G", "3 G"],
      "Load factor in a level turn is 1 divided by the cosine of the bank angle. cos 60 is 0.5, so the load factor is 2 G, and stall speed rises by about 41 percent."),
    S(1, 2, "Left-turning tendencies", "In a climb at high angle of attack and high power, P-factor makes a single-engine airplane with a clockwise-turning propeller (seen from the cockpit) tend to",
      ["yaw to the left", "yaw to the right", "pitch nose down"],
      "At high angle of attack the descending blade on the right side meets the air at a greater angle and produces more thrust, so the airplane yaws left. The pilot holds right rudder."),
    S(1, 2, "Ground effect", "An airplane taking off in ground effect",
      ["may lift off below the speed it needs to climb out of ground effect",
       "needs a higher airspeed than usual to lift off from the runway surface",
       "has more induced drag than in free air, slowing its acceleration"],
      "Within about one wingspan of the surface, ground effect reduces induced drag. The airplane may become airborne before it reaches the speed it needs to keep flying once it climbs out of ground effect."),
    S(1, 2, "Airspeed indicator", "On an airspeed indicator, what does the top of the white arc represent?",
      ["The maximum flaps extended speed (VFE)",
       "The maximum structural cruising speed (VNO)",
       "The power-off stalling speed with flaps up (VS1)"],
      "The white arc is the flap operating range: its bottom is VSO, its top VFE. The green arc runs from VS1 to VNO, and the red line is VNE."),
    S(1, 3, "Pitot-static system", "The static port becomes blocked by ice during a climb, while the pitot tube stays clear. How do the instruments respond?",
      ["The altimeter stays at the same reading and the vertical speed indicator shows zero",
       "The altimeter keeps working normally but the airspeed indicator drops straight to zero",
       "The vertical speed indicator shows a climb and the altimeter reads too low"],
      "With a blocked static source the altimeter freezes at the altitude where the blockage happened and the VSI reads zero. The airspeed indicator still works but becomes inaccurate (it reads low in a climb)."),
    S(1, 2, "Carburetor ice", "In an airplane with a fixed-pitch propeller, the first sign of carburetor ice is usually",
      ["a loss of RPM", "a rise in oil temperature", "a rise in manifold pressure"],
      "Ice in the carburetor restricts airflow, so with a fixed-pitch propeller the first indication is a drop in RPM, often followed by rough running. With a constant-speed propeller it shows as a drop in manifold pressure."),
    S(1, 2, "Magnetic compass", "In the Northern Hemisphere, on a heading of west, you accelerate. What does the magnetic compass show?",
      ["A turn toward the north", "A turn toward the south", "No change in heading"],
      "Acceleration error on east or west headings follows ANDS: accelerate north, decelerate south. Accelerating on a west heading the compass swings briefly toward north."),
    S(1, 3, "Center of gravity", "How does a center of gravity located forward of the limit affect an airplane?",
      ["It raises the stall speed and makes the nose harder to raise for landing",
       "It lowers the stall speed and makes the airplane less stable in pitch at all speeds",
       "It improves the cruise speed and makes stall recovery more difficult"],
      "A forward CG needs more tail-down force, which acts like extra weight, so stall speed rises and cruise speed drops. It also makes rotation and the landing flare harder. An aft CG makes the airplane less stable and stall recovery harder."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 2, "Maneuvering speed", "How does design maneuvering speed (VA) change as the airplane's weight decreases?",
      ["It decreases", "It increases", "It stays the same"],
      "A lighter airplane reaches its limit load factor at a lower speed, because a gust changes its angle of attack more easily. So VA drops as weight decreases."),
    S(2, 1, "Airspeed indicator", "What does the yellow arc on an airspeed indicator mark?",
      ["The caution range, flown only in smooth air",
       "The normal operating range with the flaps retracted",
       "The full flap operating range for landing"],
      "The yellow arc runs from VNO to VNE. Fly in it only in smooth air and with caution. The green arc is the normal operating range and the white arc the flap range."),
    S(2, 2, "Spins", "What has happened in a spin?",
      ["Both wings are stalled, one more deeply than the other",
       "Only one wing is stalled while the other is still flying",
       "Neither wing is stalled, but the airplane is out of trim"],
      "A spin is an aggravated stall with autorotation: both wings are stalled, with one at a higher angle of attack. Recovery starts with power to idle, ailerons neutral, full opposite rudder and the elevator forward to break the stall."),
    S(2, 2, "Altimeter", "You fly from an area of high pressure into one of lower pressure without resetting the altimeter. Compared to your indicated altitude, your true altitude will be",
      ["lower", "higher", "the same"],
      "Without resetting, the altimeter reads higher than the airplane really is when pressure drops along the route: high to low, look out below."),
    S(2, 3, "Detonation", "Which conditions are most likely to cause detonation in a piston engine?",
      ["Too lean a mixture and very high cylinder temperatures",
       "Too rich a mixture and very low cylinder temperatures",
       "A high-octane fuel grade used at a low power setting"],
      "Detonation is the explosive burning of the fuel-air charge, caused by high temperatures, an overly lean mixture, too low a fuel grade or high power with poor cooling. Richening the mixture and lowering the nose help cool the engine."),
    S(2, 1, "Fuel", "What color is 100LL aviation gasoline?",
      ["Blue", "Green", "Red"],
      "100LL is dyed blue, grade 100 is green, and jet fuel is clear or straw colored. Never use a lower grade than the minimum the manufacturer approves."),
    S(2, 2, "Gyroscopic instruments", "Why must the heading indicator be checked against the magnetic compass in flight?",
      ["Precession makes it drift from the correct heading",
       "It shows true headings rather than magnetic ones",
       "Magnetic dip causes it to lead in turns to the north"],
      "The heading indicator is a gyro with no magnetic sensing. Precession from friction makes it drift, so it should be reset to the compass in straight and level, unaccelerated flight, about every 15 minutes."),
    S(2, 3, "Electrical system", "During flight the ammeter shows a large discharge and the low-voltage light comes on. What has most likely happened?",
      ["The alternator has failed and the battery is supplying the load",
       "The battery is overcharging and the alternator should be reset",
       "The magnetos have failed and the engine is running on the battery"],
      "A discharge with a low-voltage warning means the alternator is no longer charging and the battery is carrying the electrical load. Shed electrical load and land at the nearest suitable airport. Magnetos are independent of the electrical system."),
    S(2, 2, "Magnetos", "Why should the ignition switch be treated as live, and the propeller as able to start, even with the key off?",
      ["A broken magneto ground wire can leave the magneto able to fire",
       "The battery can turn the starter even when the master switch is off",
       "The fuel injection system keeps pressure after the engine stops"],
      "The magneto is grounded through the P-lead when switched off. If the P-lead breaks or comes loose, the magneto stays hot and turning the propeller can start the engine."),
    S(0, 2, "Adverse yaw", "When a pilot rolls into a left turn with aileron alone, the nose tends to yaw to the right. Why?",
      ["The rising right wing makes more lift and more induced drag",
       "The left aileron goes up and adds parasite drag to the left wing",
       "Torque from the engine pushes the nose toward the high wing"],
      "Adverse yaw comes from the induced drag of the rising wing, whose downward aileron makes more lift. Coordinated rudder in the direction of the turn corrects it."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 1, "Four forces", "In steady, unaccelerated, level flight",
      ["lift equals weight and thrust equals drag",
       "lift is greater than weight and thrust equals drag",
       "thrust is greater than drag and lift equals weight"],
      "In straight and level, unaccelerated flight the opposing forces balance: lift equals weight and thrust equals drag."),
    S(0, 2, "Stability", "Longitudinal stability is about which axis?",
      ["The lateral axis", "The longitudinal axis", "The vertical axis"],
      "Longitudinal stability is stability in pitch, which is movement about the lateral axis (wingtip to wingtip). Lateral stability is roll about the longitudinal axis."),
    S(0, 2, "Load factor", "What are the limit load factors for an airplane in the normal category?",
      ["Plus 3.8 G and minus 1.52 G", "Plus 4.4 G and minus 1.76 G", "Plus 6.0 G and minus 3.0 G"],
      "Normal category airplanes are designed for plus 3.8 and minus 1.52 G, utility category for plus 4.4 and minus 1.76 G, and acrobatic category for plus 6.0 and minus 3.0 G."),
    S(0, 2, "Flaps", "How does lowering the flaps change the airplane's performance?",
      ["It increases lift and drag and lowers the stall speed",
       "It increases lift, reduces drag and raises the stall speed",
       "It reduces lift and drag and raises the stall speed"],
      "Flaps increase the wing's camber, giving more lift and more drag at a given speed. The stall speed drops, which allows a steeper, slower approach."),
    S(0, 2, "Carburetor ice", "In which conditions is carburetor ice most likely?",
      ["Temperatures between about 20 and 70 degrees F with visible moisture or high humidity",
       "Temperatures below about 0 degrees F with clear skies and very low relative humidity at the surface",
       "Only in clouds, at outside air temperatures below about 32 degrees F"],
      "Fuel vaporization and the venturi can cool the carburetor by up to about 70 degrees F, so ice can form between about 20 and 70 degrees F with high humidity, even in clear air."),
    S(0, 3, "Carburetor heat", "What happens when carburetor heat is applied?",
      ["The fuel-air mixture becomes richer", "The fuel-air mixture becomes leaner", "The engine RPM always rises at once"],
      "Heated air is less dense, so the same fuel flow mixes with less air and the mixture richens. With a fixed-pitch propeller RPM first drops, then rises if ice was present."),
    S(0, 2, "Mixture", "Why must the mixture be leaned as an airplane climbs?",
      ["The air becomes less dense, so the mixture becomes too rich",
       "The air becomes denser with height, so the mixture becomes too lean",
       "Fuel pressure rises with height and floods the carburetor"],
      "As altitude increases air density drops while the carburetor still meters fuel by volume, so the mixture gets richer. Leaning restores the right ratio and avoids rough running and fouled plugs."),
    S(0, 2, "Pitot-static system", "If the alternate static source inside the cabin is used, the altimeter will usually read",
      ["slightly higher than normal", "slightly lower than normal", "exactly the same as with the normal source"],
      "Cabin pressure is usually a little lower than outside static pressure because of the venturi effect of air flowing around the cabin, so the altimeter and airspeed indicator read slightly high."),
    S(0, 3, "Pitot-static system", "The pitot tube and its drain hole become fully blocked by ice. As the airplane climbs, the airspeed indicator",
      ["shows an increase, acting like an altimeter",
       "shows a decrease, falling toward zero at once",
       "keeps working normally from the static port"],
      "With the ram air inlet and drain hole both blocked, pressure is trapped in the pitot line. As the airplane climbs and static pressure drops, the indicated airspeed rises, so the instrument behaves like an altimeter."),
    S(0, 2, "Magnetic compass", "In the Northern Hemisphere, turning from a heading of north, the magnetic compass will initially",
      ["show a turn in the opposite direction", "lead the turn by a large amount", "show the correct heading at all times"],
      "Northerly turning error (from magnetic dip) makes the compass lag in turns from a north heading, first swinging the opposite way, and lead in turns from a south heading."),
    S(0, 1, "Propellers", "Why is a propeller blade twisted along its length?",
      ["To keep a more even angle of attack from hub to tip",
       "To make the blade tips turn more slowly than the blade roots at the hub",
       "To reduce noise at the highest power settings"],
      "The tip moves much faster than the hub, so the blade angle decreases toward the tip to give a fairly even angle of attack and thrust along the blade."),
]
